A||||10||dennis_6||Timing on non running motor.||||1||
Z||000000||dennis_6||11-28-2001||12:46 AM||||I suspect the timing is way off on my Fiero, but I can not get it to start.  How do I set the timing with out using a gun.  Engine will not run at all and I need to make sure the distributor is firing the right plug at the right time.  Anyone know how to do this?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||clayatandalusia||11-28-2001||12:48 AM||||are you sure you have the right wireing order it's in the intake - batt side<P>Clay Brown <P>85' Med Royal Blue 2M4 SE 5 Speed<BR>84' White 2M4 4 Speed<BR>87' Red 2M6 5 Speed<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||dennis_6||11-28-2001||01:04 AM||||My wiring order is fine.  What is in the intake?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Neal||11-28-2001||01:04 AM||||Your best bet is to get the motor to TDC #1 firing position.  How to do this with the valve cover on? i dunno<P>Now you go to the distributor pick the terminal the rotor is closest to fireing, now rotate the distributor so the rotor is sitting just AFTER the terminal (because we want to fire before TDC so when the motor is at TDC the rotor will be past fireing position by the amount of advance).  We want about 10* advance in our cars so if you can figure out 10* after the firing position it will be close, close enought to start and fine tune anyways.<P>Also make sure of you plug wire routing is in order.<BR>are you getting spark at all?||||reg||1
Z||000004||dennis_6||11-28-2001||01:12 AM||||Spark is good, air is good, fuel is good.  Engine cranks, but does not start.  It makes a muffled backfiring sound.  Only thing left is timing.  Wire order is also good.  I am at alost what to do if it is not timing.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hank is Here||11-28-2001||01:20 AM||||All here sounds good but I'd like to add a few tidbits.  Set the engine to TDC.  You can do this with a helper or remote stareter switch.  Take out hte spark plug and then put your finger over the hole till your finger is pushed out by pressure.  Then set to 10deg bTDC.  Attach a CHEAP spark tester to the numeber one wire and plug, turn on the ignition switch.  Rotate the distributer till you see spark out of the tester.  When you isolate the spark the distributer is set.  <P>Note:I like to use a cheap spark tester since the engine is not runnig and these like to fire off of low voltage.  <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Neal:<BR><B>Your best bet is to get the motor to TDC #1 firing position.  How to do this with the valve cover on? i dunno<P>Now you go to the distributor pick the terminal the rotor is closest to fireing, now rotate the distributor so the rotor is sitting just AFTER the terminal (because we want to fire before TDC so when the motor is at TDC the rotor will be past fireing position by the amount of advance).  We want about 10* advance in our cars so if you can figure out 10* after the firing position it will be close, close enought to start and fine tune anyways.<P>Also make sure of you plug wire routing is in order.<BR>are you getting spark at all?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Neal||11-28-2001||01:29 AM||||Hank only problem with that is deciding if you at #1 of 4 fireing position, both are TDC but if you set it to spark #1 cyl when you should fier 4 is trouble.||||reg||1
Z||000007||dennis_6||11-28-2001||02:35 AM||||I can tell this is going to be fun.||||reg||9
Z||000008||Standard||11-28-2001||03:12 AM||||You can find TDC for #1 pretty easy.. first get a 1/2" socket that fits on the bolt for the crankshaft pulley (can't remember what size it is), and you can use a 1/2" ratchet to turn over the engine without having to mess around with a breaker bar.  Take out the #1 spark plug and put your finger in the hole.  Turn over the engine until you feel compression.  Then you can take your finger out and put a pen or something in there, turn it over till it get's to TDC.  Line the #1 terminal on the dist. cat up with the rotor and there ya go.<P>Are ya sure the timing chain hasn't snapped?  Crank the engine over with the oil filler cap off, make sure the rockers are moving.<P>&lt;edit&gt;typo&lt;/edit&gt;<BR>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">           <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 11-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||11-28-2001||07:47 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Standard:<BR><B>You can find TDC for #1 pretty easy.. first get a 1/2" socket that fits on the bolt for the crankshaft pulley (can't remember what size it is), and you can use a 1/2" ratchet to turn over the engine without having to mess around with a breaker bar.  Take out the #1 spark plug and put your finger in the hole.  Turn over the engine until you feel compression.  Then you can take your finger out and put a pen or something in there, turn it over till it get's to TDC.  Line the #1 terminal on the dist. cat up with the rotor and there ya go.<P>Are ya sure the timing chain hasn't snapped?  Crank the engine over with the oil filler cap off, make sure the rockers are moving.<P>&lt;edit&gt;typo&lt;/edit&gt;</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>First off, if you arms aren't long enough to feel the compression in #1, remove the spark plug and reinstall it loose. That way you may be able to hear hissing as the compression stroke happens. A fool-proof way is to install a compression gauge on #1 and watch for pressure.<BR>An easy way to check for the chain/gear is intact (you didn't say which engine) is to simply remove the distributor cap and watch the rotor. If it turns when cranking it isn't broken. This does not however test to see if it has 'jumped' time. (worn out chain)<P>Once you have #1 on the up coming compression stroke, stop rotating crank at 10* BTC. Insure that the rotor is pointing at #1 plug (ideal)or at the front of the engine (water pump shaft). Replace cap and verify firing order of wires on cap (going clockwise)<P>Hope this clears it up!<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 11-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||dennis_6||11-28-2001||07:43 PM||||Thanks guys.  I can do it now.||||reg||1
